.TH octosnap-record 1  OctoSnap 
.SH NAME
octosnap\-record \- Record a GIF of a rectangle for a fixed time, in this process rather than the app
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBoctosnap record\fR [\fB\-\-gif\fR] [\fB\-\-rect\fR] [\fB\-\-display\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR] [\fB\-\-fps\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-width\fR] [\fB\-\-quality\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-cursor\fR] [\fB\-o\fR|\fB\-\-output\fR] [\fB\-\-mark\-recording\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Record a GIF of a rectangle for a fixed time, in this process rather than the app
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-gif\fR
The output is a GIF, the only format there is; accepted so scripts read right
.TP
\fB\-\-rect\fR \fI<X,Y,W,H>\fR
Record exactly this region, as logical x,y,width,height. Default: the whole monitor under the pointer
.TP
\fB\-\-display\fR \fI<CONNECTOR>\fR
Which monitor, by connector name. See `octosnap monitors`
.TP
\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fI<SECONDS>\fR [default: 5]
How long to record
.TP
\fB\-\-fps\fR \fI<FPS>\fR [default: 15]
Frames per second; 0 is the screen\*(Aqs own rate, and nothing above 50 is honoured, because a GIF cannot play faster
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-width\fR \fI<PIXELS>\fR [default: 800]
The widest the GIF may be, in pixels; 0 for no cap
.TP
\fB\-\-quality\fR \fI<QUALITY>\fR [default: 80]
The encoder\*(Aqs quality, from 1 to 100
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-cursor\fR
Leave the pointer out of the recording
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Where to write the GIF. Default: `octosnap\-<date>.gif` in the current directory
.TP
\fB\-\-mark\-recording\fR
Tell the compositor this is a recording, so it shows its own indicator too
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
